    "q-102235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102235",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}+2x+1$ whose tangent has a slope of $-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+1.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+2x+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =2x+2\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=-2$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2 & =-2 \\\\ 2x & =-2-2 \\\\ 2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-2$ at the point where $x=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
